\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h3 align=\PYGZdq{}left\PYGZdq{} style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:200\PYGZpc{};font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}font color=\PYGZdq{}\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} Part 2 \PYGZam{}mdash; The Mission Brief}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/font\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/h3\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p dir=\PYGZdq{}ltr\PYGZdq{} style=\PYGZdq{}direction: ltr;text\PYGZhy{}align: justify;line\PYGZhy{}height:200\PYGZpc{};font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;font\PYGZhy{}size:medium\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}font size=3\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} Sahar pulls a chair next to Nika and explains the goal in a single sentence, namely a context\PYGZhy{}aware autocomplete that weighs every earlier character when it guesses the next one. To make the idea concrete she reaches for an analogy that Nika will never forget, describing attention as a soft dictionary lookup. In an ordinary dictionary you hold a key, you find the one matching entry, and you read its value, but in attention every position emits a \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}query\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} that is compared against the \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}key\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} of every position, and instead of picking a single match you blend all the \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}values\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} in proportion to how well the keys matched. That blending is what lets the word \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}case\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} quietly pull meaning from \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}phone\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} three tokens earlier, and it is the entire reason Transformers can model long\PYGZhy{}range context. Over the next hour Nika will implement scaled dot\PYGZhy{}product attention with her own hands, add the causal mask that forbids any position from peeking at the future, and finally give the model a sense of order through sinusoidal positional encodings. Sahar reminds her that the search team\PYGZsq{}s evaluation harness checks numbers, not prose, so every tensor must come out exactly right. By the end of the notebook Nika will hold a working single\PYGZhy{}head attention layer, the precise building block that the next notebook stacks into multi\PYGZhy{}head attention and a full Transformer block. Each part of this five\PYGZhy{}part lab builds directly on the one before it, marching step by step from the tokenizer of Part 1 toward the small trained MiniGPT that closes the series.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/font\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} }
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}div style=\PYGZdq{}text\PYGZhy{}align:center;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}img src=\PYGZdq{}images/digikala1.png\PYGZdq{} style=\PYGZdq{}width:100\PYGZpc{};\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h3 style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:200\PYGZpc{};font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}0. The architecture at a glance\PYGZlt{}/h3\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} Before writing any code, it helps to see where tonight\PYGZsq{}s work sits inside a full Transformer. The diagram below shows the original encoder\PYGZam{}ndash;decoder architecture; a GPT keeps only the right\PYGZhy{}hand \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}masked self\PYGZhy{}attention\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} column, and the single block you are about to build by hand is the very first thing inside it. Everything else in the lab \PYGZam{}mdash; multi\PYGZhy{}head attention, the feed\PYGZhy{}forward network, the residual stream \PYGZam{}mdash; wraps around this one operation.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} }
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}div style=\PYGZdq{}text\PYGZhy{}align:center;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}img src=\PYGZdq{}images/transformer\PYGZus{}full\PYGZus{}architecture.png\PYGZdq{} width=\PYGZdq{}600\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;font\PYGZhy{}size:small;color:\PYGZsh{}666;margin\PYGZhy{}top:6px;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}GPT uses the masked self\PYGZhy{}attention (decoder) column. Everything you build tonight lives inside that one block.\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h4 style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}Learning note \PYGZam{}mdash; Why attention? Mixing information between tokens\PYGZlt{}/h4\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} After tokenization and embedding, each token in a sequence is just an isolated vector that knows nothing about its neighbours. Yet the meaning of a word depends almost entirely on its context, so a useful language model must let tokens \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}exchange information\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} with one another before predicting anything. \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}Self\PYGZhy{}attention\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} is the mechanism that does exactly this: for every position it produces a new vector that is a weighted blend of the vectors at other positions, where the weights are computed from the content itself. Unlike a convolution, which can only mix neighbours inside a small fixed window, attention can connect position one to position one thousand in a single step, which is why it captures long\PYGZhy{}range dependencies so well. Unlike a recurrent network, which must process tokens one after another, attention compares all pairs of positions at once and is therefore highly parallel, a perfect match for the accelerators that \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}jax.jit\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} targets. The price is a cost that grows with the square of the sequence length, because every position looks at every other position. Understanding this mixing operation is the single most important idea in the whole lab.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} }
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}div style=\PYGZdq{}text\PYGZhy{}align:center;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}img src=\PYGZdq{}images/gpt\PYGZus{}logo.svg\PYGZdq{} style=\PYGZdq{}width:30\PYGZpc{};\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h4 style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}Learning note \PYGZam{}mdash; Query, Key, and Value: a soft dictionary lookup\PYGZlt{}/h4\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} The cleanest way to understand attention is the soft\PYGZhy{}dictionary analogy that Sahar gave Nika. From each token\PYGZsq{}s embedding we compute three different vectors with three learned projections: a \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}query\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}q\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} that represents what this position is looking for, a \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}key\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}k\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} that advertises what each position offers, and a \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}value\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}v\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} that carries the information each position will contribute. To decide how much position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}i\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} should attend to position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}j\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{}, we take the dot product of \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}q\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}i\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} with \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}k\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}j\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}: a large dot product means the query and key point in similar directions and are therefore a good match. These match scores are turned into non\PYGZhy{}negative weights that sum to one with a \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}softmax\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{}, and the output for position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}i\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} is the weighted sum of all the \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}values\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{} using those weights. A hard dictionary would put all of its weight on a single key; attention is soft because the softmax spreads the weight smoothly across every key in proportion to how well it matched. The same three projections, applied to the same input, are what make this \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}self\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{}\PYGZhy{}attention.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} }
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}div style=\PYGZdq{}text\PYGZhy{}align:center;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}img src=\PYGZdq{}images/digikala2.png\PYGZdq{} style=\PYGZdq{}width:100\PYGZpc{};\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h4 style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}Learning note \PYGZam{}mdash; Scaled dot\PYGZhy{}product attention, and why we divide by \PYGZam{}radic;d\PYGZus{}head\PYGZlt{}/h4\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} Putting the pieces together gives the famous formula Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q K\PYGZlt{}sup\PYGZgt{}T\PYGZlt{}/sup\PYGZgt{} / \PYGZam{}radic;d\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}head\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}) V. The matrix \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}Q K\PYGZlt{}sup\PYGZgt{}T\PYGZlt{}/sup\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} holds the dot product of every query with every key, so its entry (i, j) is the raw match score between positions i and j; the softmax along each row turns one row of scores into a probability distribution over the keys, and multiplying by \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}V\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} produces the blended output. The only subtle part is the division by the square root of \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}d\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}head\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}, the dimension of each query and key vector. When \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}q\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} and \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}k\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} have independent unit\PYGZhy{}variance entries, their dot product is a sum of \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}d\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}head\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} such products and therefore has variance proportional to \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}d\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}head\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}, so for large dimensions the raw scores grow large in magnitude. Feeding large scores into a softmax pushes it into a near one\PYGZhy{}hot regime where almost all the weight lands on a single key and the gradients with respect to the other scores vanish, which cripples learning. Dividing by \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}\PYGZam{}radic;d\PYGZlt{}sub\PYGZgt{}head\PYGZlt{}/sub\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} rescales the scores back to roughly unit variance, keeping the softmax in a healthy, trainable range. We will verify this variance argument empirically with a chart.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} }
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}div style=\PYGZdq{}text\PYGZhy{}align:center;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}img src=\PYGZdq{}images/softmax.png\PYGZdq{} width=\PYGZdq{}300\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;font\PYGZhy{}size:small;color:\PYGZsh{}666;margin\PYGZhy{}top:6px;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}The softmax along each row of the score matrix turns raw match scores into attention weights that sum to one.\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h4 style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}Learning note \PYGZam{}mdash; The causal mask: no peeking at the future\PYGZlt{}/h4\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} A GPT is trained to predict the next token from the tokens that came before it, so when the model is processing position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}t\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} it must be forbidden from looking at positions \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}t+1, t+2, \PYGZam{}hellip;\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} that lie in the future. Without this rule the task would be trivial and useless, because the model could simply read the answer it is supposed to predict. We enforce it with a \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}causal mask\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{}, a lower\PYGZhy{}triangular pattern of allowed positions: position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}i\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} may attend to position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}j\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} only when \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}j \PYGZam{}le; i\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}. In code the mask is a boolean matrix, and we apply it by setting the score of every forbidden pair to a very large negative number \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}before\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} the softmax. After the softmax those entries become effectively zero, so no probability mass ever flows from the future into the present. This single trick is what makes the attention \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}causal\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{}, also called masked self\PYGZhy{}attention, and it is the only structural difference between a GPT decoder block and the encoder block of the original Transformer. When you visualize the attention weights as a heatmap, the causal mask shows up as a clean dark upper triangle.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/p\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} }
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}div style=\PYGZdq{}text\PYGZhy{}align:center;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{}     \PYGZlt{}img src=\PYGZdq{}images/digikala3.png\PYGZdq{} style=\PYGZdq{}width:100\PYGZpc{};\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}/div\PYGZgt{}}

\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}h4 style=\PYGZdq{}font\PYGZhy{}family:\PYGZsq{}Vazirmatn\PYGZsq{},Tahoma,\PYGZsq{}Segoe UI\PYGZsq{},system\PYGZhy{}ui,Arial,sans\PYGZhy{}serif;color:\PYGZsh{}EF4056\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}Learning note \PYGZam{}mdash; Positional encodings: giving the model a sense of order\PYGZlt{}/h4\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} Attention as described so far has a surprising blind spot: it is \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}permutation\PYGZhy{}equivariant\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{}, meaning that if you shuffle the input tokens the outputs are shuffled the same way, because nothing in the dot\PYGZhy{}product computation depends on \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}where\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} a token sits. Yet word order obviously carries meaning, since \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}dog bites man\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} is not \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}man bites dog\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{}, so we must inject position information explicitly. The original Transformer does this with \PYGZlt{}b\PYGZgt{}sinusoidal positional encodings\PYGZlt{}/b\PYGZgt{}: for each position we build a vector of sines and cosines whose frequencies vary smoothly across the embedding dimensions, using even dimensions for sines and odd dimensions for cosines. Concretely the angle for position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}pos\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} and dimension \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}i\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} is \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}pos / 10000\PYGZlt{}sup\PYGZgt{}(2\PYGZam{}lfloor;i/2\PYGZam{}rfloor;/d)\PYGZlt{}/sup\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}, which gives high\PYGZhy{}frequency oscillations in the early dimensions and very slow ones later. This scheme is elegant because the encoding for any position is a fixed deterministic vector that needs no training, its values stay bounded in the range from \PYGZam{}minus;1 to 1, and because of trigonometric identities a fixed offset between two positions corresponds to a linear transform of their encodings, which makes relative distances easy for the model to recover. We add this positional vector to the token embedding so that each input carries both \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}what\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} the token is and \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}where\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} it sits.}
