\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} \PYGZlt{}p style=\PYGZdq{}line\PYGZhy{}height:180\PYGZpc{}; font\PYGZhy{}size:medium;\PYGZdq{}\PYGZgt{}}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZsh{} You will now build the sinusoidal positional\PYGZhy{}encoding table from the formula in the learning note. For a sequence of length \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}T\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} and model dimension \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}d\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}, the result is a \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}(T, d)\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} matrix whose row \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}pos\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} is the encoding for that position. The angle for position \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}pos\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} and dimension \PYGZlt{}i\PYGZgt{}i\PYGZlt{}/i\PYGZgt{} is \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}pos / 10000\PYGZlt{}sup\PYGZgt{}(2\PYGZam{}lfloor;i/2\PYGZam{}rfloor;/d)\PYGZlt{}/sup\PYGZgt{}\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{}; even dimensions take the sine of the angle and odd dimensions take the cosine. Because the pairing of \PYGZlt{}code\PYGZgt{}2\PYGZam{}lfloor;i/2\PYGZam{}rfloor;\PYGZlt{}/code\PYGZgt{} ties dimensions together two at a time, neighbouring sine/cosine pairs share a frequency, which is the property that makes relative positions linearly recoverable.}
